```

The full request owns immutable data for the call/deferred execution lifetime. It contains no caller-provided RHI command buffer. `RequiredResourcePolicy` names every required mesh/texture/material and any explicitly allowed placeholder; absence or failure of a required item fails the entire candidate.

`SceneUploadPlan` remains a validated transient lowering for a full snapshot, not scene identity. Its header duplicates the target authority, revision, active scene, activation epoch, root external-state serial, and projection digest from the request, and validation requires exact equality. Every mesh, texture, material, transform, instance, cull, descriptor, and draw record carries its stable source ID in parallel with any dense candidate index. Indices are bounds-checked coordinates inside that one candidate and never cross the facade boundary as durable identity. `SceneUploadPlanValidationResult` becomes a typed value with the failing stable ID/record index and error; `commitSceneUploadPlan` does not rely on assertions or silently skip a required record.

`RendererTargetFence` binds backend capability, renderer instance, device instance/generation, mapping epoch, authority, applied document revision, scene activation epoch, root external-state serial, active scene, and active projection digest.

`SceneApplyDisposition` is exhaustive: `AcceptedIncremental`, `AcceptedFullSnapshot`, `AlreadyApplied`, `Rejected`, `FailedBeforeSubmit`, `FailedAfterSubmit`. `SceneApplyError` is a P5-owned typed logical error that includes invalid schema/digest/payload, authority, stale revision, revision gap, scene activation mismatch, root serial mismatch, projection mismatch, mapping mismatch, renderer mismatch, device mismatch/loss, stale object generation, unsupported capability, allocation, descriptor staging, recording, submission, publication invariant, and retirement enqueue. `SceneApplyResult` also carries the existing backend-neutral `rhi::RHIResult` for an RHI failure; public code does not translate it into native backend status.

`SceneApplyResult` returns:

- disposition and typed error/message;
- canonical change-set digest;
- old and resulting fences (resulting fence is present only for accepted/already-applied work);
- exact queue submission tokens;
- created, logically retired, and eventually destroyed handle counts/IDs suitable for diagnostics;
- per-transaction counter before/after/delta snapshots;
- optional reason requesting a separately validated full snapshot;
- explicit placeholder-resource use, which is zero unless policy opted in before staging.

Validation errors are values, not assertions. A stale delta is rejected and may request a snapshot; it is never silently upgraded into an immediate rebuild. Metal returns `Unsupported` before renderer mutation while unavailable. The facade provides no native device, queue, command buffer, descriptor, or backend-specific escape hatch.

For a document batch, the active revision/digest must equal the first source step, every adjacent source step must close exactly, and the resulting fence must equal the last step. Missing intermediate evidence is `RevisionGap`; target revision greater than base is not by itself a gap when the complete chain is present. Scene-activation and scene-root-state changes are not folded into a document batch; they remain separately ordered requests or a complete full snapshot.

The legacy full-upload API may remain internally while migration completes, but P5 acceptance paths go through the new transaction. `waitForIdle()` is never reached by an accepted incremental transaction or rollback path.

### 7.6 Incremental effect policy

| Logical operation | Required P5 lowering | Forbidden work |
|---|---|---|
| Node local transform | Recompute affected world transforms parent-before-child; update instance transform, previous/current draw transform, cull bounds/sphere, and attached camera/light derived records. | Full rebuild, geometry/texture upload, device idle |
| Local visibility | Recompute effective subtree visibility; update instance/draw/cull participation and backend-neutral AS visibility model. | Geometry/texture upload, device idle |
| Material scalar/vector | Update exact packed material range; atomically update draw classification only when alpha/double-sided/pipeline key changes. | Geometry/texture upload, device idle |
| Existing texture/sampler binding | Resolve existing live resource generations, stage descriptor generation, publish after dependencies. | Uploading texture pixels, geometry upload, device idle |
| Primitive material/variant binding | Publish the final existing material handle to every raster/AS consumer and update bucket/classification. | Reference-path asymmetry, geometry/texture upload, device idle |
| Camera property or camera-from-view | Update camera record and, for atomic camera-from-view, the node/world dependent records in the same transaction. | Geometry/texture upload, device idle |
| Light property | Update exact packed light range; recompute position/direction from attached node if affected. | Geometry/texture upload, device idle |
| Instance add/remove | Incremental only after atomic registry/mapping publication and fault tests pass; otherwise `StructuralTopology` full snapshot. | Partial dense-remap publication |
| Hierarchy add/delete/reparent, attach/detach, split/merge | Initial P5 uses an explicit `StructuralTopology` full snapshot. | Silent incremental fallback |
| Scene switch | `SceneSwitch` full snapshot with activation epoch +1 and unchanged document revision. | Fabricated revision, clear-first replacement |
| Global Merge/Compact | Candidate document/projection plus `GlobalMerge`/`Compact` full snapshot. | Partial in-place publication |
| Geometry/texture schema/content change | Reason-coded full snapshot unless a separately scoped immutable replacement API already exists and passes the same transaction tests. | Expanding locked editor authoring scope merely to satisfy P5 |

Accepted continuous Transform, Material, Light, Visibility, Camera, and binding edits must report zero full rebuilds, zero geometry upload calls/bytes, zero texture upload calls/bytes, and zero device-idle calls.

### 7.7 Candidate generation, publication, and rollback

Both incremental and full snapshot paths use one state machine:

1. Validate all logical and renderer fences, IDs, capabilities, hashes, required-resource policy, and dependency closure with zero RHI side effects.
2. Stage copy-on-write CPU mirrors, actual-handle mapping, dirty ranges, draw/cull/light/camera records, descriptor generation, and resource journal. Active pointers remain unchanged.
3. Reuse immutable mesh/texture generations only after content hash, schema, owner, and device generation match. Topology rebuild does not imply re-uploading unchanged geometry or textures.
4. Allocate candidate resources and record work through MGIF RHI. A required failure rejects/fails the whole candidate; no partial-looking `SceneUploadResult` is accepted.
5. Submit work and capture every relevant queue token. Descriptor visibility is not published before dependencies are established.
6. Perform one nonthrowing atomic swap of active generation, renderer mapping, descriptors, revision/epochs, and digests. There is no sequence of publicly observable partial vector mutations.
7. Logically invalidate superseded handles and defer physical destruction against exact submission tokens. No rollback uses device-wide idle.
8. Commit applied counters/result only after publication.

Failure invariants:

- Validation failure: only exact rejection counter changes; zero allocation/record/submit/descriptor/retirement/idle.
- Pre-submit failure: destroy unpublished resources immediately; active generation and all fences/digests remain byte-identical.
- Post-submit/prepublication failure: retain the old active generation; orphan candidate resources retire by captured tokens.
- Retirement-enqueue failure: publication has not occurred; the resource journal retains ownership and retries enqueue without exposing handles. It may not leak, publish partially, or idle the device.
- Publication is designed `noexcept`. Any invariant failure before the swap rolls back the whole candidate. `partialPublicationCount` remains zero.
- Device loss invalidates its generation; recovery creates a new device generation and requires a separately validated full snapshot.

Normal topology/full-snapshot validation must be Vulkan Validation and synchronization-validation clean. Shutdown/device-loss drains may idle under an explicit reason counter; ordinary editing, normal full snapshots, and rollback do not.

### 7.8 Counters

Use monotonic `uint64_t` counters and return transaction-local deltas. At minimum:

- received, already-applied, accepted-incremental, accepted-full-snapshot, rejected, failed-before-submit, failed-after-submit;
- rejection buckets for schema, digest, authority, revision stale/gap, activation epoch, root serial, projection, mapping, renderer, device, generation, unsupported, payload;
- operations received/coalesced/applied by Transform, MaterialParameters, MaterialBinding, Camera, Light, Visibility, Hierarchy, Topology;
- geometry/texture/other upload calls, bytes, ranges/subresources, and submissions;
- resources created, logically retired, physically destroyed, orphaned, and retirement backlog;
- full snapshot count by reason;
- rollback, allocation failure, recording failure, submission failure, publication failure, retirement-enqueue failure, and `partialPublicationCount`;
- `deviceIdleCalls` by shutdown, device loss, surface teardown, and forbidden/other reason;
- current authority, revision, activation epoch, root serial, mapping epoch, renderer/device generation, projection digest, and active-generation digest.

Rejected and rolled-back work cannot increment applied work. `partialPublicationCount` is a sentinel whose accepted value is always zero.

